prompt:
I work in a one-bay garage where several cars are waiting for fixes; each job has an estimated service time, a promised completion time, and a seriousness rating that says how bad it is if that car goes out late. The bay can only handle one car at a time, every car needs to be done once (no skipping or doubling up), and if a repair finishes after its promised time the lateness is how many minutes it’s late while on-time or early repairs add nothing. The “score” for a schedule is found by taking each job’s minutes-late and multiplying by its seriousness, then adding those values for all cars — the plan that keeps that total pain as small as possible is the one to shoot for. Concrete details for the specific repairs are shown below.

{
  "n_repairs": 5,
  "repair_ids": [
    0,
    1,
    2,
    3,
    4
  ],
  "data": [
    {
      "repair_id": 0,
      "service_time_minutes": 35,
      "severity_weight": 5,
      "promised_completion_time_minutes": 58
    },
    {
      "repair_id": 1,
      "service_time_minutes": 54,
      "severity_weight": 6,
      "promised_completion_time_minutes": 65
    },
    {
      "repair_id": 2,
      "service_time_minutes": 63,
      "severity_weight": 1,
      "promised_completion_time_minutes": 76
    },
    {
      "repair_id": 3,
      "service_time_minutes": 33,
      "severity_weight": 2,
      "promised_completion_time_minutes": 65
    },
    {
      "repair_id": 4,
      "service_time_minutes": 11,
      "severity_weight": 8,
      "promised_completion_time_minutes": 77
    }
  ]
}

Oh, and when you send the final schedule back, please just follow this simple JSON layout so it's easy to read and parse — nothing fancy, just the ordered list of car IDs in the sequence they'll be worked on:

{
  "solution": [
    <car_id_first>,
    <car_id_second>,
    ...,
    <car_id_last>
  ]
}

"solution" is the list that shows the order the cars get the bay — first item is the car you do first, next is the one after that, and so on. Each placeholder like <car_id_first> is where the actual job identifier from the instance would go; think of it like filling in the "which car?" fields on a form.

This is just a sketch of the shape I want, not the actual answer. Please make sure to use the exact identifiers as they appear in the instance input — do not rename them or invent new labels. Valid identifiers look like plain numbers such as “1” or “23”, single capital letters like “A” or “B”, or a capital letter followed by digits like “A1” or “X7”.

We run a one-printer shop where requests pile up: each request comes with a page count, a pickup deadline, and an urgency rating. The trick is deciding which job to print first, second, and so on — the printer can’t do two jobs at once, nothing can be skipped or duplicated, and every order must be completed. To compare schedules, take each job’s lateness (how long it finishes after its promised pickup; if it’s on time or early, that’s zero), multiply that lateness by the job’s urgency, and sum those values across all jobs — the goal is to make that total as small as possible. The specific orders and timings are listed below.

