On a busy afternoon the single 3D printer has a queue of projects, each with its own print duration, a hoped-for completion time, and an importance rating that measures how bad being late is. The problem to solve is the order to run the prints — one job at a time and every project must go through once — so the overall penalty is kept small. That penalty is simply calculated by, for each project, taking how many minutes it finishes after its hoped-for time (zero if it’s not late), multiplying that lateness by the project’s importance, and summing those figures for every project. The concrete job durations, desired finish times, and importance ratings follow below.

